Hi Frederic,

Le Mon, Sep 21, 2026, Frederic Weisbecker a =C3=A9crit :
> I can't manage to reproduce that, neither on latest mainline nor on 7.2.7
>
> Can you share your whole .config file?

Attached: config-7.2.5-1-default, taken from /boot/config-$(uname -r) on an
affected node. It is the stock openSUSE Tumbleweed/MicroOS package
kernel-default-7.2.5-1.1.x86_64, unmodified, no custom build. It differs
from
config/x86_64/default in openSUSE:Factory/kernel-source at the 7.2.5 source
revision only in toolchain-detection and build-metadata lines (CC/AS/LD
version, LOCALVERSION, MODULE_SIG_KEY, CC_HAS_*), no functional option:
https://api.opensuse.org/public/source/openSUSE:Factory/kernel-source/confi=
g.tar.bz2?rev=3D4dabae0b715184e35b05b9a168bde6b7

The unaffected control box runs kernel-default-7.1.8-1.1.x86_64 from the
same
distribution, also stock, same VM type, same command line. Between the two
configs the only change among the scheduler, tick, accounting, preemption
and
RCU options is CONFIG_SCHED_CACHE=3Dy appearing in 7.2.5.

Environment, in case it is what your test box does not share:

  - Every node is a KVM guest (Hetzner Cloud, QEMU, "AMD EPYC-Rome
Processor",
    8 vCPUs, one with 4), clocksource kvm-clock.
  - No steal-time accounting at all: there is no kvm-stealtime line in the
    boot log and the steal column has read exactly 0 on every node for the
    75 hours since boot, so the hypervisor does not seem to expose the MSR.
  - CONFIG_VIRT_CPU_ACCOUNTING_GEN=3Dy, CONFIG_CONTEXT_TRACKING_IDLE=3Dy an=
d
    CONFIG_NO_HZ_FULL=3Dy are built in (SUSE default) with nohz_full=3D emp=
ty,
    so the CPUs run idle dynticks, not full.
  - CONFIG_HZ=3D1000, CONFIG_PREEMPT_DYNAMIC=3Dy, psi=3D1 on the command li=
ne:
    BOOT_IMAGE=3D/boot/vmlinuz-7.2.5-1-default root=3DUUID=3D... rd.timeout=
=3D60
    rd.retry=3D45 quiet systemd.show_status=3Dyes console=3DttyS0,115200
    console=3Dtty0 ignition.platform.id=3Dopenstack security=3Dselinux seli=
nux=3D1
    psi=3D1

It is fleet-wide, not one box. /proc/uptime idle as a percentage of
uptime x nr_cpus, read today on all eight nodes:

  node kernel cpus uptime idle
  cp-fsn1 7.2.5-1-default 8 75.0h 102.64%
  cp-nbg1-pps 7.2.5-1-default 8 74.5h 102.03%
  cp-nbg1-waa 7.2.5-1-default 8 74.2h 103.30%
  cp-hel1 7.2.5-1-default 8 75.2h 97.59%
  cp-hel1-b 7.2.5-1-default 8 75.2h 101.20%
  worker-fsn1 7.2.5-1-default 8 74.0h 100.27%
  monitoring 7.2.5-1-default 4 73.8h 102.75%
  staging 7.1.8-1-default 8 852.4h 89.15%

cp-hel1 is the busiest node and stays under 100 only because real work
displaces it; the excess is an offset on idle, not a constant.

A 60 s delta over the per-CPU cpuN lines of /proc/stat on cp-nbg1-waa
(8 cores, 480.06 CPU-second ceiling):

  user 10.84 nice 0.00 system 6.60 iowait 2.33 irq 0.00 softirq 2.65 steal
0.00
  busy 22.42 =3D 4.67% of ceiling
  idle 493.64 =3D 102.83% of ceiling
  sum 516.06 =3D 107.50% of ceiling (excess 36.00, all of it in idle)

/proc/uptime's idle field moves with /proc/stat exactly (stat_idle /
uptime_idle =3D 1.0000 on both kernels), so the two files agree with each
other and only wall clock is exceeded. On 7.1.8 the modes sum to
0.992-0.997 of the ceiling over the same kind of window.

What did you reproduce on: bare metal or a KVM guest, and with
TICK_CPU_ACCOUNTING or VIRT_CPU_ACCOUNTING_GEN? I only have the version
bisection (7.1.8 good, 7.2.5 bad, same config family), not a commit. If it
helps I can boot kernel-vanilla and a 7.2.7 build on a throwaway VM of the
same type, or run any diagnostic you want on an affected node.

Thanks,
Ahmed

